  <p>
    β-MSH — beta-melanocyte-stimulating hormone — is one of the quieter members of the
    melanocortin family, but it carries an outsized lesson about why this system matters
    in people specifically. Like <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a>, it is a broad-spectrum
    agonist cut from <a href="/system">POMC</a>. Unlike α-MSH, its story is largely a
    human one, because the animals most of melanocortin science has been done in cannot
    make it at all.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="The cut the mouse cannot make. Humans and rodents both carve ACTH and β-lipotropin out of POMC and both cut ACTH down to α-MSH. Only POMC with a pair of basic residues inside γ-lipotropin — human, not rodent — can be cut again to release β-MSH."
    source="Schematic, after Pritchard et al. 2002, Harrold et al. 2003 and Lindberg & Fricker 2021."
  >
    <CutTheMouseCannotMake />
  </Figure>

  <h2>Where it comes from: the lipotropin branch</h2>
  <p>
    POMC is a prohormone cut into pieces at pairs of basic amino acids by the
    prohormone convertases PC1/3 and PC2, and the pieces it yields depend on which
    enzymes a tissue carries.<Cite n={2} /> The first cuts release an N-terminal
    fragment, ACTH, and β-lipotropin. α-MSH is then carved from within ACTH. β-MSH
    comes from the other end of the molecule: in tissues that express PC2 — the
    hypothalamus, the skin and the pituitary's intermediate lobe — β-lipotropin is
    cut into γ-lipotropin and β-endorphin, and γ-lipotropin is cut again to release
    β-MSH, a 22-amino-acid peptide in humans that carries the family's shared
    His-Phe-Arg-Trp core.<Cite n={2} /><Cite n={8} /> It is, in other words, made by
    the same enzyme and in the same neurons as α-MSH, as a parallel product of the
    same precursor.
  </p>

  <h2>The cut the mouse cannot make</h2>
  <p>
    What makes β-MSH worth its own page is a quirk of sequence. The final cut that
    frees β-MSH from γ-lipotropin requires a pair of basic residues at the right
    spot, and rodent POMC does not have it. In mice and rats, processing stops at
    γ-lipotropin, and no β-MSH is ever produced.<Cite n={1} /><Cite n={3} /> Humans,
    other primates and dogs carry the site and make the peptide. The consequence is
    easy to overlook and important to remember: a satiety signal that is real in
    people is absent from the standard animal model. Mice get by perfectly well with
    α-MSH alone, which tells us α-MSH is sufficient for the circuit — but not that
    it is the whole of the circuit in us.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>

  <h2>A second key for the appetite receptor</h2>
  <p>
    At the receptor that matters most for body weight, β-MSH is not a weaker copy of
    α-MSH. It binds <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a> with affinity at least equal
    to α-MSH's, and when given into the brain of rats it suppresses feeding — the
    rat, lacking its own β-MSH, has no trouble reading it.<Cite n={1} /> Harrold and
    colleagues argued in 2003 that β-MSH might be the more important natural MC4R
    ligand in the human hypothalamus, where both peptides are made side by
    side.<Cite n={1} /> That remains a hypothesis rather than a settled fact, because
    the two peptides cannot be separated in a living human brain; what is settled is
    that β-MSH is a genuine part of the human melanocortin "fullness" signal, and
    the genetics that followed made that concrete.
  </p>

  <h2>A variant in the β-MSH sequence</h2>
  <p>
    In 2006 two groups, publishing back to back, reported the same rare POMC
    variant: a tyrosine-to-cysteine change, Tyr221Cys, that falls inside the β-MSH
    sequence and leaves every other POMC product untouched. Among obese children in
    the United Kingdom the variant was found far more often than in the general
    population; the children carrying it were hyperphagic and unusually tall for
    their age, the same pattern seen when MC4R itself is impaired. In the
    laboratory, the variant peptide bound MC4R less well and activated it less
    strongly than normal β-MSH.<Cite n={4} /> The second study, in German
    children, reached the same conclusion and showed that the variant peptide
    could not match the natural one in switching the receptor on.<Cite n={5} /> A
    single amino acid, in a peptide mice do not make, was enough to tilt human
    energy balance — and it did so by weakening one ligand's grip on MC4R while
    α-MSH was left intact.
  </p>

  <h2>The dog that proved the point</h2>
  <p>
    The most vivid evidence for what β-MSH does comes from an animal that, like us,
    makes it. In 2016 a 14-base-pair deletion was found in the POMC gene of Labrador
    and flat-coated retrievers, breeds notorious for their appetite. The deletion
    disrupts the end of the gene so that β-MSH and β-endorphin cannot be produced,
    and dogs carrying it were heavier, had more body fat and scored higher on food
    motivation; it was also more common in Labradors working as assistance dogs,
    where a strong food drive helps training.<Cite n={6} /> A follow-up in 2024
    showed what the missing peptides had been doing. Dogs with the deletion had a
    resting metabolic rate about a quarter lower than unaffected dogs and were
    hungrier between meals, although they did not eat faster or more at a sitting:
    the deletion lowered how much energy the body spends and raised the drive to
    seek food, the two levers the melanocortin system is known to hold.<Cite n={7} />
    It is as close to a natural knockout of β-MSH as exists, and it was found in a
    species that has the peptide to lose.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the mouse data need a translation step</h2>
  <p>
    All of this feeds one practical lesson. POMC-neuron biology, MC4R pharmacology
    and most of the appetite story on this site were worked out in mice, and in mice
    the entire melanocortin satiety signal is α-MSH. In humans it is α-MSH and β-MSH
    together, read by the same receptor. Where that matters is at the edges: in
    interpreting human POMC variants, where a change in the β-MSH region can have
    effects no mouse would show; in understanding why POMC-processing enzyme
    deficiencies look the way they do in people;<Cite n={3} /> and in reading any
    claim about a melanocortin ligand's human relevance that rests on rodent data
    alone. The receptors are shared. The set of natural keys is not.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    β-MSH is a reminder that the melanocortin system is not identical from one species
    to the next. Its broad receptor activity makes it look like a second α-MSH, but its
    human-specific role in appetite gives it a distinct place in the story, confirmed by
    genetics in two species — and a cautionary note for anyone reading mouse data as if
    it were human data. For the receptor at the centre of that appetite signalling, see{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R — the appetite rheostat</a>; for the drug that
    re-supplies the signal β-MSH and α-MSH share, see{' '}
    <a href="/setmelanotide">setmelanotide</a>.
  </p>

